ome of anthropology’s most engaging scholars. ... London: Bloomsbury 2016: 213-222.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAmerica’s Grid: Past and Uncertain Future A Review of “The Grid: the Fraying Wires Between Americans and Our Energy Future” by Gretchen Bakke (https://ena.etsmtl.ca/pluginfile.php/1473976/mod_folder/content/0/02-Documentation/Facultative/Bakke_ReviewInEnergyJournal.pdf?forcedownload=1)\nciteturn106search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Book ...  Grid: ...  Gretchen Bakke. ...  Bloomsbury, ...  (2016)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2\nElectricity is not a noun\nGretchen Bakke\nO body (https://bakkeconsolidated.org/things/Not_a_Noun.pdf)\nciteturn106search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; conditioning – the most electrically intensive items in a home (see Bakke 2016). ... Bakke, Gretchen. ... The Grid: The Fraying Wires between Americans and Our Energy ... New York: Bloomsbury.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE COGNITIVE AND THE (https://www.aixenergy.io/content/files/2026/01/The_Cognitive_Grid_and_the_Modern_Nonfiction_Canon.pdf)\nciteturn106search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Bakke, Gretchen.The Grid.New York: Bloomsbury, 2016.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConversations in Bloomsbury (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversations_in_Bloomsbury)\nciteturn106search17 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; As a text that blurs the lines between memoir, fiction, and autobiography, Conversations enacts numerous textual aspirations. ... He chooses to publish his book at a time when postcolonial critical theory is at its height, suggesting that perhaps he is deliberately satirising the revered status of the Bloomsbury Group at a time when it was popular to scrutinise modernist ideas under postcolonial light, playing on their affirmed celebrity status, made possible only by this historical gap. ... Helen Southworth, Oxford University Press 2010. ... Although most of the Bloomsbury Group are relatively kind and embracing of Anand and anti-imperialist attitudes, Gretchen Gerzina has argued that whilst 'the Bloomsbury Group may have been radicals in Edwardian English society, they were not radical enough to take seriously a viewpoint utterly removed from their own intellectual aesthetic world.'\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBill Gates Picked These Books as the Best of 2016 (https://time.com/4590348/bill-gates-best-books-of-2016/)\nciteturn106news18 [wordlim: 100] Published: 9.9 years ago; The subject of this 2014 book by international-relations scholar Archie Brown made it an especially relevant read during the recent U.S. presidential election-and earned it a spot on Gates' 2016 list. ... Honorable mention: The Grid, by Gretchen Bakke.Gretchen Bakke's The Grid had special resonance for Gates: his first job was writing software for the entity that controls the Northwest power grid.\n\"You don't have to play or even watch tennis to love this book,\" Gates writes. \"The late author wielded a pen as skillfully as Roger Federer wields a tennis racket.\"\n2. Shoe Dog, by Phil Knight.\nGates has met the author of the memoir Shoe Dog and can attest that the co-founder of Nike can be tricky to get to know. Knight's openness in the book is what won it a spot on the list. The memoir \"is a refreshingly honest reminder of what the path to business success really looks like: messy, precarious, and riddled with mistakes,\" Gates writes.\n3. The Gene, by Siddhartha Mukherjee.\nIn The Gene, oncologist and Pulitzer-winning author Siddhartha Mukherjee explains how scientists have come to understand genes and addresses the moral questions of current genome technologies. The best part? You don't need to be a doctor to understand it. \"Mukherjee wrote this book for a lay audience, because he knows that the new genome technologies are at the cusp of affecting us all in profound ways,\" Gates writes.\n4. The Myth of the Strong Leader, by Archie Brown.\nThe subject of this 2014 book by international-relations scholar Archie Brown made it an especially relevant read during the recent U.S. presidential election-and earned it a spot on Gates' 2016 list. Brown argues that best political leaders are not those we perceive to be \"strong,\" but those who can negotiate, collaborate and delegate.\nHonorable mention: The Grid, by Gretchen Bakke.\nGretchen Bakke's The Grid had special resonance for Gates: his first job was writing software for the entity that controls the Northwest power grid. The deep dive on the U.S. electric grid reveals its complexity and importance and highlights the difficulty in reinventing it for a clean-energy future. It also fits into one of Gates' favorite categories: \"Books About Mundane Stuff That Are Actually Fascinating.\"\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvery Book Recommendation Bill Gates Has Ever Published (https://time.com/4686426/bill-gates-book-recommendations/)\nciteturn106news19 [wordlim: 100] Published: 9.6 years ago; Every Book Recommendation Bill Gates Has Ever Published ... My Favorite Books of 2016 ... The Grid by Gretchen Bakke\nEvery Book Recommendation Bill Gates Has Ever Published\nDate: Thu Mar 23 15:06:40 2017 UTC\n\nBill Gates is a very busy man, but as his admirers know, he makes time to read. The Microsoft co-founder and philanthropist regularly reviews books on his website and formulates lists of his favorite books of the year, or the best ones on a given subject. Here is every book he's ever recommended on his website, from highly academic texts to The Hunger Games.\nNote: In the assorted reviews category, we left off books that had been included in other lists - but when the same book appeared on multiple reading lists, we allowed the repetition.\nMy Favorite Books of 2016\nString Theory by David Foster Wallace\nShoe Dog by Phil Knight\nThe Gene by Siddhartha Mukherjee\nThe Myth of the Strong Leader by Archie Brown\nThe Grid by Gretchen Bakke\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGunstein Bakke (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunstein_Bakke)\nciteturn106search20 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.4 years ago; Caption: Norwegian author Gunstein Bakke reading from his book Maud og Aud at an evening of literature in Bygland, Norway. ... October 2016.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGrid: A next generation data parallel C++ QCD library (https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03487)\nciteturn106academia21 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; Title: Grid: A next generation data parallel C++ QCD library ... Date: Thu Dec 10 23:51:19 2015\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGuy Mankowski (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_Mankowski)\nciteturn106search22 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; * An Honest Deceit (Urbane Publications) October 2016, 2nd Ed., 2018. ... His third novel, How I Left The National Grid, was written as part of his PhD. ... In 2015 he also edited the memoir of banking whistle-blower Paul Moore (banking manager), whose evidence about corruption at HBOS bank was presented to the UK House of Commons Treasury Select Committee.An Honest Deceit was a New Writing North Read Regional 2018 title, which lead to a tour of English libraries. ... Bloomsbury Publishing. ... - Guy Mankowski: How I Left the National Grid (Book launch), - the Journal.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nR. Gregory Christie (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R._Gregory_Christie)\nciteturn106search23 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; * One Last Word: Wisdom from the Harlem Renaissance (Bloomsbury, 2017) ... * 